    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Overview"># [icon:cloud24] Intune

The **Intune** tab defines one or more Win32 app packages that will be uploaded to Intune when the bundle is run. Package metadata here layers on top of the shared **General** tab (icon, installer, identity).

## Toolbar

- [btn:Add] - creates a new Intune package in the current bundle. The new package is populated from the selected template in the **Template** dropdown (or a blank baseline if no template is selected).
- [btn:Remove] - deletes the currently selected package from the bundle.
- [btn:Duplicate] - creates a copy of the selected package so you can vary one field (e.g., a 32-bit vs 64-bit variant) without re-filling every setting.
- [btn:Assignments] - opens the assignments editor for the selected package (groups, intent, filters). The amber badge counts assignment warnings (incomplete targeting, duplicate group targets); the red badge counts blocking errors. The same amber/red pair appears on the package list rows and the nav rail, so a problem is traceable from the rail down to the exact field.
- **Template** - selects which preset the [btn:Add] button will use. Templates are created from an existing package via **Save as template** and stored in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Wrapp\Templates` - no package templates ship built-in, so the list starts empty until you (or an org template pack) save one. A template can also carry the package's **assignments**: check *Assignments* in the save dialog and the dropdown shows a badge with the count - applying such a template replaces the target package's assignments wholesale. Picking a template *after* [btn:Add] does not change the existing package - it only seeds the next one you add.

## Templates in practice

A behavioral template might set:

```ini
InstallExperience = system
RestartBehavior   = basedOnReturnCode
MaximumInstallationTimeInMinutes = 60
AppName = {{Company}} {{Name}}
```

`{{Token}}` placeholders (see Settings &gt; Placeholders) stay literal in the saved template and expand against the current bundle when the template is applied - in scalar fields, in collection entries, and in any assignments the template carries.

## Target Tenants (collapsible)

The collapsible **Intune Tenants** section under the toolbar controls which tenants this *specific bundle* is deployed to when it runs. Unchecked tenants are skipped. See the info button inside that section for more detail on the **Add Tenant** and **Restore...** controls.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Toolbar.Add">Add a new Intune package, seeded from the currently selected Template.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Toolbar.Remove">Remove the selected Intune package from this bundle.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Toolbar.Duplicate">Duplicate the selected Intune package so you can tweak a single field without refilling every value.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Toolbar.Assignments">Open the assignments editor for the selected package (groups, intent, filters). The amber badge counts assignment warnings (incomplete or duplicate targeting); the red badge counts blocking errors.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.Toolbar.Template">Template used to seed new packages when Add is clicked. Save a configured package as a template to add your own (no package templates ship built-in - the list starts empty). A template can also carry the package's ASSIGNMENTS: check "Assignments" in the save dialog, and the dropdown shows a badge with the count. Applying such a template replaces the target package's assignments; placeholders like {{Name}} expand on apply. Changing this dropdown does not modify existing packages.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.TenantsPanel">The **Intune Tenants** section controls which tenants this bundle is deployed to at run time.

## Tenant rows

Each row represents one Intune tenant with its display **Name**, tenant **Domain**, and tenant **Key** (the GUID tenant ID). Uncheck a row to skip that tenant for this bundle only. At least one tenant must stay selected for the bundle to be processable.

## Add Tenant

Inserts a blank tenant row that you can fill in for this bundle. Use this when a tenant is unique to this package and you don't want to pollute your saved Preferences.

## Restore...

A dropdown that copies tenants from another source into this bundle's list. It edits the in-memory bundle only - **Save Bundle** is still required to persist.

- **Overwrite with Preferences** - replaces the bundle's tenant list with the one saved in Settings &gt; Preferences. Destructive; confirmation is required.
- **Add missing from Preferences** - adds only tenants from Preferences whose tenant ID isn't already in the bundle. Non-destructive.
- **Overwrite with App Defaults** - replaces the list with the tenants shipped as defaults with Wrapp. Destructive.
- **Add missing from App Defaults** - adds only default tenants whose tenant ID isn't already in the bundle. Non-destructive.

**Preferences** are your personal saved tenant list in Settings. **App Defaults** are the baseline list shipped with Wrapp and never change across updates unless the app itself is updated.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.TenantsPanel.Add">Add a blank tenant row to this bundle (does not modify saved Preferences).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.TenantsPanel.Restore">Copy tenants from Preferences or App Defaults into this bundle. In-memory only until Save Bundle.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Intune.TargetTenants">Select which Intune tenants this package should be deployed to.

Unchecked tenants are skipped during the run. At least one tenant must be selected for the package to be processed.

Packages are filtered per-tenant at run time. Each tenant receives only the packages that target it.</sys:String>

