- **Gotchas:** every field referenced in the format becomes **required at save time** - a format using `{Language}` refuses to save a bundle with an empty Language. The preview under the field resolves against the current App Info.

## Icon Folder Name

- **What it does:** the subfolder created inside each bundle for the extracted icon PNG. Stored as `IconFolderName`; default `Icon`. Applies to bundles saved after the change.

## PSADT Template Path

- **What it does:** overrides the bundled PSADT v4.1.8 template for new PSADT bundles. Stored as `PsadtTemplatePath`; empty = use the bundled template.

## Reset buttons (true scopes)

| Button | Resets | Leaves alone |
|--------|--------|--------------|
| Reset Defaults | Directory format, icon folder, PSADT path, theme, entire Key Vault tab, Update feed + mode | Tenants, sites, domains, packaging defaults |
| Reset Templates | Built-in script/package templates back to shipped content | Your custom templates |
| Reset Intune Tenants / SCCM Sites / Domains | That one list (secrets forgotten for tenants) | Everything else |
| Reset All | Moves settings.json to a timestamped **backup** and reloads factory defaults in place | MSAL sign-in cache, vault-stored encryption keys, templates |</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Preferences.Domains">Saved domain paths that seed new bundles' `Domain` section.

## Columns

| Column | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| Key | Name of the domain entry. Becomes the JSON **object key** under the bundle's `Domain` root in Config.json (e.g. `"Domain": { "corp.contoso.com": { ... } }`). |
| IsDistPath | Distribution/source path for the domain (UNC). |
| AppFolder | Where the domain expects application content. |
| TagFolder | The domain's tag/log folder. |

## Where they take effect

- **Stored where:** `Domains` in settings.json.
- **New bundles** copy this list into their Config.json `Domain` section at creation. Existing bundles keep their own list until you use **Sync Domains** on the JSON view.
- **Used by:** the Appease scripts' transcript-copy step, which resolves the machine's domain against these entries to decide where logs land.
- **Gotchas:** removing rows here never touches existing bundles.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Endpoint">Paths the generated scripts use **on target machines**.

## Fields

| Field | Token | Default | Stored where |
|-------|-------|---------|--------------|
| Tag folder | `{{TagFolder}}` | `C:\Wrapp\Tag` | `EndpointTagFolder` |
| Local app folder | `{{LocalAppFolder}}` | `C:\Wrapp\Apps` | `EndpointLocalAppFolder` |

## Where they take effect

The tokens are expanded into the bundle's scripts when the `Script/` folder is written: the Detect script's `DetectionResults.json` path, the Appease scripts' logs/cache locations, and the Install script's `$LocalAppDir`. They are also exported to `user-defaults.json` so the PowerShell module sees the same values (CLI parity).

## Gotchas

- Changing these affects **only bundles created (or re-scripted) afterwards** - existing bundles keep the paths already baked into their scripts.
- These are endpoint-side paths; they have nothing to do with where bundles are saved on your machine.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.KeyVault">Configuration for the Azure DevOps git repo that stores `.intunewin` encryption keys.

## Fields

| Field | Stored where | Notes |
|-------|--------------|-------|
| Enable Azure DevOps Key Vault | `EnableAzureDevOpsKeyVault` | Master gate. Off = every vault read/write silently no-ops. |
| Repo URL | `KeyVaultRepoUrl` | `https://dev.azure.com/{org}/{project}/_git/{repo}` format. |
| Key path template | `KeyVaultPathTemplate` | Auto-captured keys. Default `/wrapp/{Tenant}/{AppId}.json`. |
| Manual key path template | `KeyVaultManualPathTemplate` | Tools-saved keys (no tenant/app ID). Default `/manual/{PackageName}.json`. |
| Use pull requests | `KeyVaultUsePullRequests` | On = branch + PR per save instead of direct commits to main. |
| PR branch / title / description templates | `KeyVaultPrSourceBranchTemplate` etc. | Only used in PR mode. |

**Tokens** (all template fields): `{Tenant}` `{AppId}` `{AppName}` `{PackageName}` `{Date}` `{Author}`. Token values are sanitized - they can't inject extra folders; literal `/` in the template are the folder boundaries. Live previews under each field show the resolved shape.

## Trust approval (TOFU)

A new or changed repo URL triggers an approval prompt **when you Save**. The approval is a machine-bound token: both reads and pushes refuse an unapproved URL, so an edited settings.json or imported config can't silently redirect key material. Declining just leaves the URL unapproved.

## Gotchas

- **Custom path template caveat:** fast key lookups read the hard-coded legacy path `/wrapp/{tenant}/{appId}.json`. Keys saved under a custom template are found only by the recursive crawl (brute force / batch flows) - lookups still work, just slower.
- **PR mode:** one PR per saved key (no batching), and the repo must already have a `main` branch - PR mode cannot seed an empty repo.
- The PR description template supports multi-line text; `\n` in the stored value becomes a real newline in the PR body.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Updates">Where and how Wrapp updates itself.

## Update feed

- **What it does:** the location Wrapp checks for new versions (a Velopack release feed).
- **Stored where:** `UpdateFeedUrl` in settings.json; org-seedable via a defaults file.
- **Accepted values:** an `https://` URL, a UNC share (`\\server\share\wrapp`), or a local folder (`C:\wrapp\releases`). Plain `http://` is **rejected** - updates are executable code.
- **Trust:** a new feed needs a one-time approval on this machine (prompt on Save). Unapproved feeds are never contacted.

## Update mode

Stored as `UpdateMode`.

| Mode | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| `Auto` | Checks at launch; an available update lights the **action-needed indicator** in the status bar. Updating is always your call - it never interrupts open work. |
| `NotifyOnly` | Same indicator-based notification; intended for fleets that update Wrapp through Intune/SCCM supersedence. |
| `Disabled` | No automatic checks. |

Orgs that push Wrapp via Intune/SCCM supersedence should seed `NotifyOnly` or `Disabled`.

## Check / install flow

**Check for updates** runs against the **saved** values - Save first if you just edited the feed. Start the install from the action-needed indicator or this page: Wrapp first runs the normal save prompts (Cancel keeps the session open and the update stays pending), then switches to the **update screen**. Open work always comes first: any other open Wrapp windows are *asked* to close with their own save prompts, and **only after every window has closed** does the download begin - step-based progress (downloading, rebuilding, applying), the version you're moving to, package size, and the full feed hash for verification. Wrapp then restarts on the new version. On a fresh launch with no other Wrapp windows open, a pending update is offered right on the splash screen before any work begins.

## Gotchas

- Update checks are a no-op for non-installed runs (dev builds, portable folder) - only a real install has an updater.
- The What's-New popup after an update, and the version cards in About, come from this same channel.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Defaults.OrgImport">Imports an organization defaults file - the same JSON the first-run wizard accepts - at any time.

## Semantics: merge, never overwrite

Each value the file covers is applied **only if your setting is still at its factory default**. Your explicit choices are never overwritten - importing is always safe. If nothing was at factory default, the import still succeeds: the file is stored and becomes this profile's defaults source.

## The file becomes a durable fallback

The imported file is copied to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Wrapp\defaults.local.json`, where it survives app updates. From then on its tenants, sites, and domains fill the Preferences tables **whenever those tables are empty** (e.g. after a Reset), and its redaction patterns are applied to the logs.

## Forcing a value from the file

One escape hatch: **clear that field** in Settings (returning it to empty/factory) and re-import. Or use *Import settings* below, which replaces the whole profile instead of merging.

## Never seeded

Client secrets (per-user DPAPI) and trust approvals (Key Vault URL, update feed) are deliberately not seedable - a pre-filled URL still triggers the per-user approval prompt.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Defaults.Portability">Export / import of your whole profile - settings and preferences - as one JSON file.

## Export

Writes everything: paths, tenants, sites, domains, Key Vault and update settings, and all packaging defaults. **Stripped on export:** client secrets, the Key Vault and update-feed trust tokens, gate state (liability waiver, first-run answers), and version markers. They are per-machine/per-user values that would be useless - or an attack surface - anywhere else.

## Import

Replaces your current configuration wholesale (confirmation first). **Preserved from this machine:** stored client secrets, Key Vault and update-feed approvals, and your liability-waiver acceptance - an import can never grant trust or leak a secret. Tip: export your current settings first if you might want them back.

## Using an export as an org defaults file

An exported file *mostly* works as an organization defaults file: tenants, sites, domains, and the packaging default blocks all map. **Caveat:** the export's flat `KeyVault*`, `Endpoint*`, and `Update*` properties do **not** map - an org defaults file expects those under nested `Vault` / `Endpoint` / `Update` blocks. Add those blocks by hand if your org baseline needs them.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders"># [icon:rename24] Placeholders

Placeholders are `{{name}}` tokens that expand into script templates, package metadata defaults, and anywhere you use **Replace placeholders**. Double braces are used because single braces occur naturally in PowerShell and JSON; `{{name}}` never does.

## Built-in vs custom

| Kind     | Rows            | Behavior |
|----------|-----------------|----------|
| Built-in | The grayed rows | Reserved names resolved **live** from the current bundle (`{{Name}}`, `{{Company}}`, versions, installer files) plus computed values (`{{Date}}`, `{{Author}}`) and the endpoint folders from Settings. Always replaced - even when empty. Cannot be edited or shadowed. |
| Custom   | Your rows       | Any name you define: group IDs, share paths, org URLs. Names are `A-Z a-z 0-9 - _` (max 64), unique (case-insensitive), and may not collide with a built-in. A duplicate, reserved, or invalid name is flagged red and **blocks saving Settings** until fixed. |

## Replacement rules

- Known name with a value → **replaced** (single pass, values inserted verbatim - a value containing `{{x}}` stays literal).
- Custom name with an **empty** value → left as a visible `{{todo}}` in place.
- Unknown name → left untouched.

## Sensitive placeholders

Tick **Sensitive** for values that should not sit in plain text (tokens, secret paths). The value is DPAPI-encrypted into `placeholders.secure.json` beside settings.json on Save: readable only by your Windows user on this machine, never included in exports or org defaults files, and the plaintext is scrubbed from every log line. The grid shows `(stored)` instead of the value; typing a new value replaces the stored one on the next Save.

Custom placeholders travel with **Export settings** (sensitive rows export name-only) and can be seeded by an organization defaults file (add-missing, never overwriting yours).</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Add">Add a custom placeholder row. Give it a name (letters, digits, - and _), a value, and optionally mark it Sensitive.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Remove">Delete every custom placeholder row with its checkbox ticked. A removed sensitive placeholder's encrypted value is deleted from this machine on Save.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Name">The token name - used as `{{name}}` in templates and fields. Letters, digits, - and _ only (max 64), case-insensitive, unique, and never one of the built-in names. A duplicate, reserved, or invalid name is outlined in red and blocks saving Settings until corrected or removed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Value">What `{{name}}` expands to. Built-in rows show the live value for the current bundle. An empty custom value leaves the token literal in place (a visible TODO). Sensitive values are typed masked and stored encrypted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Sensitive">Store this value DPAPI-encrypted in placeholders.secure.json instead of settings.json: machine-bound, excluded from exports and org files, and scrubbed from logs. Replacing a sensitive placeholder into a bundle still writes its plaintext there - you are warned first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Placeholders.Comment">Operator note for this placeholder (e.g. "Intune pilot group id"). Exported with the row; never expanded.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.EffectiveConfig">Read-only view of the configuration actually in effect on this machine - organization policy, your preferences, and the organization defaults file.

## Organization policy

When your organization manages Wrapp via policy, a table at the top lists every mandated value with its source (machine or user policy), plus provisioned tenants/sites/domains, placeholders, hidden sections, and redaction patterns. Mandated values are enforced at launch, on every save, and after any import; change them via Group Policy / Intune / Apply-WrappPolicy.ps1, then restart Wrapp.

## Active preferences

Your settings.json rendered exactly as **Export settings** would write it - client secrets, trust tokens, and gate state stripped. What you see here is safe to share. The path row above the viewer shows exactly where it is read from, with the standard open-in-Explorer and copy-path buttons.

## Active organization defaults

The raw content of the resolved `defaults.local.json` and the path it was loaded from (same open/copy path row; the buttons disable when no org defaults file exists). This is the file that seeds tenants, sites, domains, placeholders, and redaction patterns.

Both viewers are snapshots - use **Refresh** after saving or importing.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Redaction">Every log line is scrubbed **before** it reaches disk or the Logs view. Three layers:

1. **Built-in scrubbers** (always on, organization-agnostic): JWTs, Bearer/Basic authorization headers, `client_secret` values, OAuth form tokens, and DevOps `pat=` query strings become `***REDACTED***`.
2. **Organization patterns**: case-insensitive regexes from your organization defaults file's `SensitivePatterns` array become `***ORG-REDACTED***`. The source file is listed here - Wrapp has no hardcoded knowledge of your organization.
3. **Sensitive placeholder values**: the decrypted value of every sensitive placeholder is registered literally, so it can never appear in a log - even after being replaced into a command line that gets logged.

This card is read-only; org patterns change via the organization defaults file, sensitive values via the Placeholders table above.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.VerboseUiTrace">Opt-in diagnostics for freeze and stall reports. When enabled, Wrapp writes a `[TRACE]` line to app.log for every navigation, click (with the containing view), dialog, and view build - so when the UI stalls, the log shows exactly what led up to it, alongside the automatic `[STALL]` line.

Applies immediately on Save (no restart). Leave it **off** in normal use: the trace is chatty and shortens how far back the rotated logs reach. Log redaction applies to trace lines like any other.</sys:String>

