    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Scripts.TemplateTokens">Template tokens are placeholders in script templates that get replaced with values from the current App Info when the template is applied.

## Available tokens

| Token           | Expands to                                          |
|-----------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| `{{Company}}`   | Publisher / manufacturer name                       |
| `{{Name}}`      | Application display name                            |
| `{{DotVersion}}`| Version in dotted notation (e.g. `1.2.3`)           |
| `{{Version}}`   | Version with underscores (e.g. `1_2_3`)             |
| `{{Language}}`  | Language code (e.g. `en-US`)                        |
| `{{EXEFile}}`   | EXE installer filename                              |
| `{{MSIFile}}`   | MSI/MSP installer filename                          |
| `{{GUID}}`      | Application `AppeaseGUID`                           |
| `{{Date}}`      | Current date in `yyyy-MM-dd` (resolved at apply time) |
| `{{Author}}`    | Current Windows username (resolved at apply time)   |
| `{{TagFolder}}` | Endpoint Tag folder from Settings &gt; Endpoint (default `C:\Wrapp\Tag`) |
| `{{LocalAppFolder}}` | Endpoint local app folder from Settings &gt; Endpoint (default `C:\Wrapp\Apps`) |

## Usage

Use double curly braces in any script template. When the template is applied via the **Template** dropdown, each token is replaced with the corresponding App Info value. Tokens without a value are replaced with an empty string.

## Example

**Template:**

```powershell
Set-Content -Path "{{Company}}_{{Name}}_{{DotVersion}}.log"
```

**Result** (after applying with Company=`Contoso`, Name=`AppName`, DotVersion=`1.2.3`):

```powershell
Set-Content -Path "Contoso_AppName_1.2.3.log"
```

## Saving templates: Save vs Save As

The two save icons next to the **Template** dropdown work like a standard editor:

- [btn:Save] - overwrites the *current* template in one click: the custom template you last applied or saved on this tab. Hover the icon to see exactly which template a click will overwrite. When there is no current template (nothing applied yet, it was deleted, or it is a built-in), Save behaves like Save As.
- [btn:Save As] - always prompts for a name and description and creates a new template. Afterwards it becomes the current template, so following Saves go to it.

What gets saved is the **live editor content** - you do **not** need to save the bundle first, and unsaved typing is included.

**Tokens are saved literally.** Content is stored exactly as shown in the editor: a `{{Name}}` you typed stays `{{Name}}` in the template and expands only when the template is applied. Nothing is converted back into tokens automatically - if you want a reusable template, put the tokens into the script yourself before saving. Note that applying a template expands its tokens into the editor, so re-saving previously applied content stores the expanded values unless you re-tokenize.

**Built-in templates are protected.** They can never be overwritten (pick a different name) or deleted; your own templates can be overwritten after a confirmation and deleted via the **Delete Template** option shown when you select one in the dropdown. Built-ins you have not modified are refreshed by app updates; your custom templates are never touched.

The same Save / Save As pair exists on the Intune and SCCM views for package templates (with a field picker deciding which settings the template stores), and assignments / deployments can be saved as templates from their row buttons.

## Template files

Templates live at:

```text
%LOCALAPPDATA%\Wrapp\Templates\
```

You can edit existing templates or add new `.ps1` / `.json` files. They are bootstrapped from built-in defaults on first run.</sys:String>

