    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Settings.Overview"># [icon:settings24] Settings

The **Settings** view is your personal control panel for the app, organised as a vertical rail of **ten tabs**.

## Tabs

| Tab          | What lives there                                                                          |
|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| General      | Application theme.                                                                        |
| Bundle       | Directory format, icon folder name, PSADT template path, and the Reset buttons.           |
| Domains      | Saved domain paths that seed new bundles' `Domain` section.                               |
| Endpoint     | The `{{TagFolder}}` / `{{LocalAppFolder}}` paths baked into generated scripts.            |
| Intune       | Saved tenants plus Intune package / metadata / assignment defaults.                       |
| SCCM         | Saved sites plus SCCM package / metadata / deployment defaults.                           |
| Key Vault    | Azure DevOps encryption-key vault: enable switch, repo URL, path and PR templates.        |
| Updates      | Application update feed and update mode.                                                  |
| Placeholders | The `{{name}}` placeholder table: live built-in values plus your custom placeholders.     |
| Provisioning | Organization defaults import; full settings export / import; the effective-configuration viewers and the log-redaction summary. |

## Managed by your organization

When your environment administrator manages Wrapp via policy, a **Managed by your organization** banner appears in the Settings header. Managed settings stay **visible but locked**: the control is disabled with a padlock and an explanatory tooltip - you can see the enforced value and every option, but not change it. A padlock on a **tab header** means something inside that tab is managed; padlocked **rows** in the tenant/site/domain tables are provisioned entries you can neither edit nor remove (your own entries alongside them stay fully yours). The complete list of what is enforced - and where each value comes from - lives in **Provisioning &gt; Effective configuration**. Entire tabs or views your organization has hidden simply don't appear.

## Save model

All edits are **in-memory until you click the single Save button** in the save strip at the bottom of the window. Wrapp compares the in-memory state against the last saved snapshot (re-checked every ~0.75 s), so the dirty indicator clears itself if you revert an edit by hand. Only **Theme** applies immediately - as a live preview; it too is persisted only on Save. Nothing here requires a restart.

Saving also runs the one-time trust prompts: a newly entered Key Vault URL or update feed must be explicitly approved on this machine before it is used.

## Two directions

Preferences seed **new** bundles automatically. Existing bundles pull from Preferences on demand via the **Restore...** menus on the Intune / SCCM views and the **Sync Domains** menu on the JSON view. The reverse direction exists too: the **Pull from bundle** buttons on the Intune and SCCM tabs here copy the open bundle's tenants / sites into your saved Preferences (add-missing + fill-blanks, never overwriting).

## Files

Preferences are persisted to:

    %LOCALAPPDATA%\Wrapp\settings.json

The path row at the top of the view links straight there in Explorer.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Detection.Overview"># [icon:eyetracking20] Detection

The **Detection** tab defines how Intune / SCCM decide whether the application is already installed.

## How it works

Each row below defines a **named test** - a specific check against the filesystem, registry, or a PowerShell command. Tests are combined with -and / -or / -not in the **Boolean Expression** field to form the final detection rule.

When the expression evaluates to TRUE, Intune / SCCM considers the app installed and skips installation.

## Test anatomy

| Field        | Purpose                                                                              |
|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Name         | Human-readable label for the test.                                                   |
| Symbol       | Unique identifier referenced in the Boolean Expression (auto-assigned A-Z).          |
| Path         | File path or registry key to check. Mutually exclusive with Command.                 |
| Command      | PowerShell command whose output is evaluated. Mutually exclusive with Path.          |
| Property     | File property (Exists, Version, Size) or registry value name.                        |
| Operator     | PowerShell comparison operator (-eq, -ne, -ge, -like, -match, ...).                  |
| Value        | Expected value to compare against.                                                   |

## Runtime

On each endpoint, detection results are written to `DetectionResults.json` inside the **Tag folder** - configurable in Settings &gt; Endpoint (shipped default `C:\Wrapp\Tag`) and baked into the script when the bundle is created. Exit code 0 with no stdout = not installed; any stdout output = installed. Running the script with `-VerboseMode` prints every test result but breaks true/false detection - console troubleshooting only.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.GitHistory.Overview"># [icon:history24] History

The **History** tab shows every commit on this bundle's internal git repo.

## What's tracked

Every `Save Bundle` commits the bundle folder (Config.json, scripts, icon, detection XML, etc.) to a git repository initialised inside the bundle root. History is local-only - not pushed to a remote.

## Actions

- **Refresh** - re-read the commit log from disk.
- Click a commit to see the diff and list of changed files.
- From a specific file (Config.json, install script, etc.), use the **History** button on that view to see per-file history and restore a past version. Restored content is loaded back into the editor in-memory; Save Bundle to persist.

## Why git?

Bundle state is complex and multi-file. Git gives you a cheap, local, time-ordered record so you can always step back to a known-good state without needing an external backup tool.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Tools.Overview"># [icon:wrench24] Tools

The **Tools** view collects standalone utilities that operate on `.intunewin` packages or batches of files outside the main bundle workflow.

## Tabs

- **Intunewin Decrypt** - take an encrypted `.intunewin` file (or a raw downloaded content blob) and recover its inner content. Five key sources: embedded (from the package's own `detection.xml`), manual Base64 paste, vault lookup by tenant + app ID, vault brute force across every saved key, or a CSV key list.
- **Intunewin Inspect** - show a `.intunewin` file's metadata and encryption keys without extracting the content, with **Copy Keys** and **Save Keys to Vault** actions.
- **Batch Inspect** - scan a folder (optionally recursively) full of `.intunewin` files at once, then push the discovered keys to the vault in one go. There is no CSV export - CSV files are a *decrypt input* only.

## Key vault

The vault is **not** a local store - it is the **Azure DevOps git repository** configured in Settings &gt; Key Vault. The feature is gated: it can be switched off entirely, and the repo URL must be approved once per machine (a trust prompt on Save) before any key is read from or pushed to it. The repo's access control is the security boundary; keys are never cached per-workstation. See the Vault section help for the folder layout and read paths.

## Drag and drop

Drop a `.intunewin` file onto the Decrypt or Inspect tab to populate the input path automatically (Decrypt also auto-loads embedded keys). Folder drops work on the Batch tab.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Tools.Decrypt">Recovers the plaintext content of an encrypted `.intunewin` package or a raw content blob downloaded from Intune.

## What's inside the file

The encrypted payload has a fixed 48-byte header ahead of the ciphertext:

```
[ HMAC-SHA256 (32 B) ][ IV (16 B) ][ AES-256-CBC ciphertext, PKCS7 padding ]
```

When a MAC key is available (embedded and vault sources), the HMAC is **verified before anything is decrypted** - a tampered blob or wrong MAC key refuses to decrypt. Manual, brute-force, and CSV sources have no MAC key, so they validate the *output* instead (known file signatures: ZIP, MSI, EXE, CAB).

## Key sources

| Source          | Where the key comes from                                   | Needed before Decrypt enables            |
|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| Embedded        | The package's own `detection.xml` (auto-loaded on drop/browse) | Key + IV populated automatically     |
| Manual          | Base64 key + IV you paste                                  | Both Key and IV fields                   |
| Vault lookup    | The DevOps vault entry for a tenant + app ID               | Tenant ID and App ID                     |
| Vault brute force | Every key in the vault, tried fastest-first              | Nothing (vault must be enabled)          |
| CSV key list    | A CSV file of key/IV pairs, tried in order                 | CSV file path                            |

Brute force and CSV first quick-test each key against only the first cipher block (near-instant), then fully decrypt with the winner. The winning key/IV is shown back in the Manual fields so you can note it.

## CSV format

Header row + one pair per line. Column aliases: `EncryptionKey` or `Key`, and `InitializationVector` or `IV`. Values inside quotes must not contain commas.

```
EncryptionKey,InitializationVector
bXkta2V5LWJ5dGVzLWhlcmUtMzItYnl0ZXMtbG9uZyE=,bXktaXYtMTYtYnl0ZXMhAA==
YW5vdGhlci1rZXktZm9yLWEtc2Vjb25kLWFwcCEhIQ==,c2Vjb25kLWl2LWJ5dGVzIQ==
```

## Output

You pick an output folder up front. If the decrypted result is a ZIP (the usual case for a wrapped bundle) it is **extracted into a subfolder** named after the file; otherwise the file's real type is sniffed from its header and it is renamed with the right extension (`.msi`, `.exe`, `.cab`, `.zip`). Name collisions get a `_2` suffix instead of overwriting. Progress and per-step outcomes appear in the Background Jobs popup.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Tools.Inspect">Reads a `.intunewin` package's metadata and encryption keys without extracting its content.

## What it reads

Every `.intunewin` is a ZIP containing a `detection.xml` (found anywhere in the archive) that carries the package identity **and its encryption keys in plain Base64** - which is why treating `.intunewin` files as secrets matters. A fake-value example with the exact element names Wrapp reads:

```xml
&lt;ApplicationInfo xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" ToolVersion="1.8.6.0"&gt;
  &lt;Name&gt;ContosoApp&lt;/Name&gt;
  &lt;UnencryptedContentSize&gt;18874368&lt;/UnencryptedContentSize&gt;
  &lt;FileName&gt;IntunePackage.intunewin&lt;/FileName&gt;
  &lt;SetupFile&gt;Script\InstallScript.ps1&lt;/SetupFile&gt;
  &lt;EncryptionInfo&gt;
    &lt;EncryptionKey&gt;QUFBQS1mYWtlLWtleS1ub3QtcmVhbC0zMi1ieXRlcyE=&lt;/EncryptionKey&gt;
    &lt;macKey&gt;QkJCQi1mYWtlLW1hYy1rZXktbm90LXJlYWwtMzJCIQ==&lt;/macKey&gt;
    &lt;InitializationVector&gt;Q0NDQy1mYWtlLWl2LTE2QiE=&lt;/InitializationVector&gt;
    &lt;mac&gt;RERERC1mYWtlLWhtYWMtdmFsdWUtbm90LXJlYWwhIQ==&lt;/mac&gt;
    &lt;profileIdentifier&gt;ProfileVersion1&lt;/profileIdentifier&gt;
    &lt;fileDigest&gt;RUVFRS1mYWtlLXNoYTI1Ni1kaWdlc3QtdmFsdWUhIQ==&lt;/fileDigest&gt;
    &lt;fileDigestAlgorithm&gt;SHA256&lt;/fileDigestAlgorithm&gt;
  &lt;/EncryptionInfo&gt;
&lt;/ApplicationInfo&gt;
```

The mixed casing (`EncryptionKey` PascalCase, `macKey` / `mac` / `fileDigest` camelCase) is real - that's what Microsoft's wrapper emits; Wrapp reads the elements case-insensitively.

## Why Inspect takes a few seconds

Besides reading `detection.xml` (instant), Inspect **decrypts the inner content in a temp folder and looks for the bundle's `Config.json`** to propose a good vault name (`Name_Version`). Falls back to installer metadata, then the setup filename. The suggestion is editable before saving.

## Actions

- **Copy Keys** - copies the full key record to the clipboard as JSON.
- **Save Keys to Vault** - pushes the record to the DevOps vault under the manual path (default `/manual/&lt;name&gt;.json`), after checking for collisions: an identical key/IV already in the vault is skipped; a same-name file with a *different* key/IV offers Overwrite / Save as copy (`_2` suffix) / Skip. Requires the vault to be enabled and configured in Settings &gt; Key Vault.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Tools.Batch">Scans a folder of `.intunewin` files and pushes all newly-found encryption keys to the vault in one pass.

## Scanning

Pick a folder (or drop one on the tab) and click Scan. The **recursive** toggle includes every subfolder. Each file is inspected like the Inspect tab does - including the inner-content decrypt that resolves a proper `Name_Version` vault name - so expect a few seconds per file. Progress is cancellable and tracked in the Background Jobs popup.

## Row outcomes

| Status        | Meaning                                                                            |
|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `OK`          | Keys found and **not** in the vault yet - will be pushed by Save.                  |
| `Exists`      | The same key + IV pair is already in the vault (the matching entry is shown).      |
| `No metadata` | No readable `detection.xml` / no embedded keys - nothing to save.                  |

The **Vault Name** cell is editable per row before saving. **Save Batch to Vault** pushes only the `OK` rows; each push runs the same collision checks as single-save (skip identical, prompt on same-name-different-key). There is no CSV export.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Tools.Vault">Where Wrapp keeps `.intunewin` encryption keys: the **Azure DevOps git repository** configured in Settings &gt; Key Vault. One JSON file per key set.

## Folder layout

```
/
├── wrapp/                                     auto-captured during packaging runs
│   └── 11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555/  tenant ID
│       ├── aaaa0000-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeee00000001.json   Intune app ID
│       └── aaaa0000-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeee00000002.json
├── manual/                                    saved from the Tools view
│   ├── ContosoApp_1_2_3.json
│   └── ContosoApp_1_2_3_2.json                _2 suffix from a name collision
└── README.md                                  ignored by Wrapp
```

`/wrapp/{Tenant}/{AppId}.json` and `/manual/{PackageName}.json` are the *default* path templates - both are configurable in Settings &gt; Key Vault.

## The two read paths (honesty section)

1. **Fast lookup** - fetching keys for a known tenant + app ID reads the **hard-coded legacy path** `/wrapp/{tenant}/{appId}.json` with a single request. Keys saved under a *custom* path template are invisible to this path.
2. **Recursive crawl** - vault brute force (and batch dedup) lists the repo recursively and reads **every `.json` file at any depth**, so custom-template keys are always found this way; brute force stops at the first key that decrypts. If a fast lookup misses, flows cascade to the crawl automatically.

## Vault file schema

```json
{
  "AppId": "aaaa0000-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeee00000001",
  "TenantId": "11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555",
  "DisplayName": "Contoso App",
  "PackageName": "ContosoApp_1_2_3",
  "EncryptionKey": "QUFBQS1mYWtlLWtleS1ub3QtcmVhbC0zMi1ieXRlcyE=",
  "InitializationVector": "Q0NDQy1mYWtlLWl2LTE2QiE=",
  "MacKey": "QkJCQi1mYWtlLW1hYy1rZXktbm90LXJlYWwtMzJCIQ==",
  "Mac": "RERERC1mYWtlLWhtYWMtdmFsdWUtbm90LXJlYWwhIQ==",
  "FileDigest": "RUVFRS1mYWtlLXNoYTI1Ni1kaWdlc3QtdmFsdWUhIQ==",
  "FileDigestAlgorithm": "SHA256",
  "SetupFile": "Script\\InstallScript.ps1",
  "UnencryptedContentSize": 18874368,
  "SavedAt": "2026-08-11T15:04:05.000Z",
  "SavedBy": "packager1"
}
```

## Trust and PR mode

- **TOFU approval:** the repo URL must be approved once per machine (prompt on Settings Save). Both **reads and pushes** refuse an unapproved URL - a tampered settings.json cannot silently redirect key material.
- **PR mode:** when *Use pull requests* is on, every save creates a feature branch + pull request instead of committing to `main` directly - one PR per key. Requires the repo to already have a `main` branch.
- Overwriting an existing vault file keeps the old key recoverable in the repo's **git history** - treat a replaced key as compromised and rotate if that matters.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.Overview"># [icon:boxarrowup24] Run

The **Run** tab kicks off real packaging runs against Intune or SCCM for the currently-open bundle.

## Toolbar

- [btn:Start] - runs the pre-flight checks (duplicate-name guard, then the **collision check** against each signed-in tenant), shows the **Deployment Plan** confirmation dialog, and only then spawns the background packaging job. The run is tracked in the Background Jobs popup; you can navigate to other views during a run and the job continues.
- [btn:Cancel] - signals the PowerShell runspace to stop at the next safe point.
- [btn:Open Log Folder] (top-right of the view) - opens the app log folder in Explorer. Always available, independent of whether a run is active; the path to the folder is shown beside the icon.

## Target + mode

- **Target** (Intune / SCCM / Both) - determines which packager module runs and which tenants or sites are consulted.
- **Mode** (Validate Only / Package Only / Full Run) - *Validate Only* performs dry-run checks with no tenant calls; *Package Only* builds the `.intunewin` but doesn't upload; *Full Run* builds, uploads, and assigns/deploys.

## Per-package progress

The progress table below the toolbar shows each package's step in real time.

- **Intune:** Collision Check &#x2192; Wrapping &#x2192; App Creation &#x2192; Dependencies &#x2192; Assignment
- **SCCM:** Collision Check &#x2192; Detection Script &#x2192; App Creation &#x2192; Content Distribution

Packages can be individually enabled or disabled on the Intune / SCCM views (see the checkbox tooltip there); a disabled package stays in the bundle but is skipped by the whole run, shown as [badge:Skipped:gray] with the reason **Disabled**.

Outcome badges appear when each package finishes:

- [badge:Done:green] - succeeded
- [badge:Partial:amber] - partial success (some steps failed)
- [badge:Failed:red] - failed (check logs)
- [badge:Skipped:gray] - skipped, with a reason: package disabled, no tenant/site selected, or tenant/site not enabled or connected
- [badge:Cancelled:lgray] - cancelled by user

## Logs

During a run, the **path of the active package log** appears above the live log stream at the bottom of the view - click it to jump straight to that file in Explorer. One log file is written per run.

When no run is active, use the [btn:Open Log Folder] button at the top-right of this view to open the app log folder directly; the folder path is always shown beside the icon.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.Targets">Pick what the run targets and how far it goes.

## Target and mode

- **Target** - `Intune`, `SCCM`, or `Both`. Decides which packager runs and which connection cards below are consulted.
- **Mode** - `Validate Only` (dry-run checks, no tenant calls), `Package Only` (builds the `.intunewin`, no upload), `Full Run` (build, upload, assign/deploy).

## Connection cards

One card per tenant (Intune) or site (SCCM) shows its sign-in state. Click a disconnected card to sign in via MSAL. Connected cards show a **token countdown** - the remaining lifetime of the cached access token; it refreshes automatically when a run needs it. The per-card checkbox enables or skips that tenant/site for this run only, without editing the bundle.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.Progress">Live per-package progress for the current run.

Each row is one package, with a step-by-step progress bar:

- **Intune:** Collision Check → Wrapping → App Creation → Dependencies → Assignment
- **SCCM:** Collision Check → Detection Script → App Creation → Content Distribution

Rows that will not run show [badge:Skipped:gray] up front with the reason: **Disabled** (package unchecked on its view), **No tenant selected** / **No site selected**, or the targeted tenant/site is not enabled or connected. Outcome badges (Done / Partial / Failed / Cancelled) replace the bar as each package finishes.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.Results">Summary of the finished run.

Shows counts per outcome (succeeded / failed / skipped packages across tenants and sites) plus the path of the run's log file - click it to jump to the file in Explorer. The same result summary and the full tenant → package → assignment tree stay available afterwards on the run's card in the Background Jobs popup.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.CollisionCheck">Before anything is wrapped or uploaded, Wrapp asks each **signed-in** tenant whether any enabled Intune package's display name already exists there (one query per tenant, using that tenant's token). A name collision becomes a decision you make up front instead of a failure you watch happen ten minutes into a run.

## The three outcomes

| Choice | What it does |
|--------|--------------|
| **Switch to Update** | Sets each colliding package's Update Mode to `Update` and fills its Existing App ID with the found app - the run updates the existing app instead of creating a duplicate. **Save the bundle afterwards to keep this change.** |
| **Skip these packages** | Disables the colliding packages (see the package Enabled checkbox help); everything else proceeds. |
| **Cancel run** | Nothing is mutated; the run stops. Closing the dialog means Cancel - never a silent default. |

"Proceed anyway" is deliberately not offered: the packaging module would take only the non-colliding packages and fail the rest, so it would be a lie.

## Fails open

Not signed in to a tenant, no enabled Intune packages, or a query error never blocks the run - the check is skipped for that tenant and the packaging module performs its own authoritative collision check during the run instead.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Run.PlanDialog">The **Deployment Plan** dialog is the last gate before every run: a tree of what is about to happen - each tenant/site, the packages targeting it (with their mode badges), and every assignment or deployment that will be applied.

**Proceed** starts the background job; **Cancel** returns you to the Run view with nothing changed. The plan is snapshotted at this moment, so the same tree is shown on the run's Background Jobs card even if you edit the bundle while the run is in flight.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Logs.Overview"># [icon:documentbulletlist24] Logs

The application log - everything Wrapp does in this session and previous ones: startup, bundle operations, packaging phases, Graph/DevOps calls, warnings and errors.

## Redaction

Messages are scrubbed **before** they reach disk or this view: bearer tokens, JWTs, and `ClientSecret=` values are always masked, and an organization defaults file can add its own sensitive patterns (tenant GUIDs, server names, domains) that are masked too.

## Files and rotation

The log lives in the app data folder as `app.log`. When it exceeds 1 MB it rotates: `app.log` → `app.1.log`, older generations shift up to `app.5.log`, and the oldest is deleted. If a second Wrapp instance runs at the same time, it writes to its own `app.pid&lt;N&gt;.log` file instead (stale instance logs are cleaned up after a week).

Use the open-folder button in the header to jump to the log folder in Explorer.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Scripts.Overview"># [icon:codetext20] Scripts

The **Scripts** tab is the in-app code editor for the bundle's install, uninstall, and detection scripts. It adapts to whichever script framework was chosen when the bundle was created.

## Toolbar

- [btn:History] - opens the git history for the currently-open script file. Restore a past version into the editor; Save Bundle persists.
- **Tab strip** - one tab per script file for the bundle's framework. **Appease** bundles show `InstallScript.ps1`, `UninstallScript.ps1`, and `DetectScript.ps1`; **PSADT** bundles show `Invoke-AppDeployToolkit.ps1` and `DetectScript.ps1`. Switching tabs preserves in-memory edits.
- **Template** dropdown (top right) - inserts a pre-written script template at the cursor. Tokens like `{{Company}}` and `{{Name}}` expand to the current App Info values at insert time. Click the info icon beside the dropdown for the full token list.

## Script framework

The editor surface adapts to the framework chosen on the General tab when the bundle was created. **The framework cannot be changed after creation - re-create the bundle to switch.**

- **Appease v2.3** - three separate files: `InstallScript.ps1`, `UninstallScript.ps1`, `DetectScript.ps1` in the `Script/` folder. Installers live in `B/`.
- **PSADT v4.1.8** - a single `Invoke-AppDeployToolkit.ps1` with Install, Uninstall, and Repair phases selected via `-DeploymentType`. Installers live in `Files/`.

## Editor

Monaco editor with PowerShell syntax highlighting and code folding (JSON, XML, and plain-text highlighting are used by the other editor surfaces). Unsaved edits persist across tab switches in-memory; hit **Save Bundle** on the bottom bar to write to disk.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.Configuration.Overview"># [icon:codetextedit20] JSON

The **JSON** tab is the raw JSON editor for the bundle's `Script/Config.json` - the single source of truth for everything the form-based tabs (General, Intune, SCCM, Detection, Scripts) edit.

## Toolbar

- [btn:History] - opens the git history for this file. Restore a past version into the editor; Save Bundle persists.
- [btn:Sync Domains] - pulls the bundle's `Config.Domain` section from Settings &#x2192; Preferences or the app's shipped defaults. Four options: overwrite-with-prefs, add-missing-from-prefs, import-from-defaults (destructive), add-missing-from-defaults. Only touches the Domain section.
- [btn:Apply Changes] - parses the editor JSON and pushes it back into the form VMs. [btn:Apply Changes] followed by Save Bundle is the persist path.

## How it relates to the form tabs

The form tabs and this editor view the same underlying `AppConfigModel`. Edits in the form tabs propagate here automatically. Edits typed here stay in the editor until you click [btn:Apply Changes].

## Editor

Monaco JSON editor with syntax highlighting, schema-aware completions where available, and fold ranges. The editor is empty until a bundle is open.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="Help.BackgroundJobs.Overview">The **Background Jobs** popup tracks every long-running operation across the session - packaging runs, inventory refreshes, MSAL token exchanges, bundle saves, key-vault syncs, batch inspects, decrypts, and application update downloads.

## Why it exists

Wrapp runs everything destructive or slow on a background thread so the UI stays responsive. Jobs are registered at kick-off and completed when they finish (or fail, or are cancelled). The popup is a single place to see in-flight work plus recent history, without digging into logs.

## What you see

Each job card shows:

- **Title** - e.g. *Packaging: Intune (FullRun) - Dell Command | Endpoint Configure*.
- **State badge** - one of the badges below.
- **Timestamps** - started + completed.
- **Result summary** (on completion) - e.g. *3/4 packages across 2 tenants, 1 failed; 7 assignments applied*.
- **Expandable deployment plan** (packaging jobs only) - the same tenant &#x2192; package &#x2192; assignment tree shown in the pre-run confirmation dialog, with per-package outcome badges.

## Job State Badges

- [badge:Running:blue] - operation is in progress.
- [badge:Done:green] - completed successfully.
- [badge:Error:red] - the operation failed.

## Package Outcome Badges

When a packaging run finishes, each package gets an outcome badge:

- [badge:Done:green] - package deployed successfully to the tenant.
- [badge:Failed:red] - one or more steps failed (check logs).
- [badge:Partial:amber] - some steps succeeded, others failed.
- [badge:Skipped:gray] - package was skipped (package disabled, tenant unchecked, or no tenant/site targeted).
- [badge:Cancelled:lgray] - cancelled by user before completion.
- [badge:Running:blue] - still in progress.
- [badge:Pending:slate] - queued, waiting for earlier packages.

## Package Mode Badges

Each package in the deployment plan shows its mode:

- [badge:Create:blue] - new app, uploaded for the first time.
- [badge:Update:purple] - updating metadata on an existing app.
- [badge:Upgrade:green] - replacing content (new `.intunewin` upload).

## Filtering and history

- Jobs are scoped to the active bundle; app-wide jobs (inventory fetches, cross-bundle tools) always show.
- History is retained across the session, bounded at 200 jobs to keep memory sensible.
- **Clear completed** in the popup header removes finished cards - in-flight jobs stay.

## Status bar badge

The floating badge in the bottom status bar reflects aggregate state of the active bundle's jobs: [badge:Running:amber] while any job is running, [badge:Error:red] if the most recent packaging run had failures, [badge:Done:green] otherwise.</sys:String>

